In-Reply-To: <DiQl5ROpMoc.AMOuBJd5_gQJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <DiQl5ROpMoc.uWqKjUHf9DgJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <DiQl5ROpMoc.AMOuBJd5_gQJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/DiQl5ROpMoc/m/1Bjh2DNoJJkJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Might there also be some middle way? Could we use sockets or something to pass
data between Python/R and C++? The maintainability problem isn't with calling
C++ code from Python or R per se, it's all the additional C++ plumbing (shared
by pystan and rstan) to construct the sampler arguments and hold the results.

re: memory. PyStan is creating copies of the data anyway in order to do parallel
sampling (which I think should be the default).

Best,

-a

On 12/03, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Tuesday, December 3, 2013 12:45:03 AM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> >
> > On 12/2/13, 7:45 PM, Ross Boylan wrote:
> > > On Sat, 2013-11-30 at 12:26 -0500, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > >> Also, anything with std::cout, std::cin, or std::cerr
> > >> hardcoded needs to be rewritten for RStan and PyStan.
> > >
> > > In python having subprocesses that do both input and output can be a bit
> > > challenging, at least if the sequence isn't known.
> >
> > Rather than a subprocess (though I'm not sure what that is),
> > I was imagining something where R or Python writes the data
> > to file from R or Python, a separate process is executed,
> > which reads from that file and writes to disk, then R or Python
> > read it back in.
> >
> > But anyway, it sounds like nobody wants to do it this way.
> > So we'll see how long it remains maintainable in the present
> > configuration of calling the Stan C++ code directly.
> >
>
> I'm not that opposed to changing that aspect of it. The only potential
> benefit of the way we were doing it would be memory, and if we are making
> copies anyway, then we don't save any memory relative to writing everything
> to the disk. It would be easier to update RStan.
>
> Ben
