Subject: triangle model
Message-ID: <NuR7rFB9mgg.J5kh61Wqx0kJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/NuR7rFB9mgg/m/J5kh61Wqx0kJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all.  Just for laffs I tried the triangle model using the untransformed parameterization as given in the manual, i.e., the following model:

parameters {
  real theta;
}
model {
  increment_log_prob (log (fmax (0, 1-fabs(theta))));
}

I put this in the file triangle2.stan and ran the following in R:

library ("rstan")
library ("rstan")
triangle_fit2 <- stan (file="triangle2.stan", iter = 10, chains = 4)
triangle_fit2a <- stan (fit=triangle_fit2, iter = 100000, chains = 4)
print (triangle_fit2a)
plot (triangle_fit2a)
theta <- extract (triangle_fit2a)$theta
hist (theta, breaks=seq(-1,1,.05))

I didn't start with 100000 sims but I needed that many to see what was going on!  The histogram looks surprisingly triangular!   The only problems show up after .95, as can be seen here:

hist (theta[theta>.9], breaks=seq(.9,1,.01))

Anyway, this surprised me.  I'd recalled (wrongly, I suppose) that the problems with the triangle occurred much closer in, more like 0.8.  I didn't realize that the problems were only right in the corners.  (And, I suppose, if I were to start the chains in a corner, they'd take forever to escape.)

A

